---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={`About ${SITE.title} — method, sources, corrections, and how ideas are submitted to government.`}>
  <article class="pt-12 pb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-ink leading-tight">
      About this journal
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
      {SITE.title} is one citizen's public record of India's progress and its
      possibilities. It has three views — <a href="/then" class="text-navy underline underline-offset-2">Then</a>,
      <a href="/now" class="text-navy underline underline-offset-2">Now</a>, and
      <a href="/next" class="text-navy underline underline-offset-2">Next</a> — because a
      credible argument about the future has to show its work about the past.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-10 anchor-link" id="method">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Method</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Every chart starts at 1947</strong>, or at the earliest year
          its data exists. The point is trajectory, not a flattering window.
          Milestone annotations mark policy events, never election years —
          the site's credibility shouldn't depend on any party's timeline.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every figure is sourced and dated</strong> on the page that
          uses it, with a link and a "data as of" date. Datasets live in the
          repo as JSON, so a figure can be traced from chart to source.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unverified is labelled unverified.</strong> Where a claim
          couldn't be independently confirmed (e.g. the status of a specific
          park project), the text says so rather than laundering it into
          fact.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ideas get an anatomy, not a wish.</strong> Each idea under
          Next states where it was seen (spark), what the gap actually is
          (with corrections — India is often further along than the
          imported framing assumes), what already exists nearby
          (precedent), and a concrete, pilot-scale ask.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Retrospective milestones end at 2014</strong> — the
          post-2014 period is told in Now's data instead, where the
          acceleration is visible on every chart.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 anchor-link" id="submission-strategy">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">
        How ideas are submitted to government
      </h2>
      <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
        A journal page doesn't reach anyone in government by itself — it has
        to be filed where it counts. The strategy, per idea:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2.5 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Respond to open consultations</strong> (ministry draft
          policies, e.g. a draft National Tourism Policy) — a comment on an
          open consultation is read; a cold submission usually isn't.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Match the channel to the idea</strong> — tourism ideas to
          the Ministry of Tourism and its MyGov campaigns; payments ideas to
          RBI/NPCI consultation windows; city-infrastructure ideas to state
          urban development departments and municipal corporations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CPGRAMS for a trackable reference</strong> — the Centralized
          Public Grievance Redress and Monitoring System issues registration
          numbers that can be followed up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>RTI as follow-up</strong> — if a submission goes
          unanswered, a Right to Information application asking what action
          was taken is cheap and legally must be answered. The reply (either
          way) becomes part of the record here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Archive everything</strong> — each submission is recorded
          with platform, date, reference number, and an archived copy;
          personal details (phone, address) are redacted from anything
          published.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fog italic">
        Portal URLs and current consultation windows are verified at filing
        time, not cached here — government portals reorganise often.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 anchor-link" id="corrections">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Corrections</h2>
      <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
        This journal errs on the side of citability. If a figure, date, or
        framing is wrong, it gets fixed visibly — corrections are noted on
        the page, not silently overwritten. The best way to report one is the
        repo's issue tracker (see README), or via the contact channels on
        <a
          href="https://blog.anindyadey.com"
          class="text-navy underline underline-offset-2">the author's blog</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 anchor-link" id="author">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Author</h2>
      <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
        {SITE.author} — born 1992, writing from the vantage of a generation
        that grew up inside the changes this journal charts. Day job:
        software. Longer writing lives at
        <a href="https://blog.anindyadey.com" class="text-navy underline underline-offset-2">
          blog.anindyadey.com</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
